tags: Reference docs
title: Custom Markup Security
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

! Threat model

Marker tiddlers (tagged <<tag $:/tags/CustomMarkup/Marker>>) and `\custom` pragmas are author content. Anyone who can save tiddlers in your wiki can author markers. In a single-user wiki this is self-attack territory; in a shared wiki it is a real attack surface. Custom Markup applies a small set of secure-by-default rules so authoring a marker tiddler does not bypass TW's normal sanitization.

! What TW core already enforces

These protections are in TW core (`$:/core/modules/widgets/element.js`) and apply to every element rendered through the parse tree, including Custom Markup markers:

|! Vector |! Mitigation |
|`<script>` tag |Renamed to `<safe-script>`. Browser does not execute. |
|Inline event handler attributes (`onclick`, `onerror`, ...) |Stripped via `excludeEventAttributes: true` when attributes are assigned to the DOM node. |
|Non-alphanumeric characters in tag name |Stripped before element creation. |

These cover the most obvious XSS vectors but do not reach attribute values that the browser later parses as HTML (most notably the iframe `srcdoc` attribute).

! What Custom Markup adds (v1.0.0-beta-23)

The plugin applies three secure-by-default rules during marker registration in `registry.js`. They apply to both marker tiddlers (the `element` and `attributes` fields) and to `\custom` pragmas (the `_element` and other parameters).

!! 1. Iframe gets a sandbox

A marker with `element: iframe` is allowed to render, but the plugin injects `sandbox=""` into its attributes if the marker author has not set a sandbox value. The empty-string sandbox is the most restrictive setting and blocks:

* Script execution (including scripts inside `srcdoc`)
* Form submission
* Top-level navigation
* Same-origin access for the iframe's document
* Plugin loading, modal dialogs, pointer lock, popups

If the marker explicitly sets a sandbox value, the plugin respects it. For example, `attributes: {"sandbox": "allow-scripts"}` lets scripts run inside the iframe but in a unique origin. This lets you embed a sandboxed widget that does need scripts.

!! 2. Some elements are denied

Markers cannot set `element` to any of:

* `object`
* `embed`
* `frame`
* `frameset`

These do not have a useful sandbox semantic, and their normal use cases (legacy plugins, frameset layouts) are obsolete. If a marker sets `element` to one of these, the field is cleared and the marker falls back to its default wrapper (`<div>` for block, `<span>` for inline).

!! 3. `javascript:` URLs are stripped

For URL-bearing attributes (`src`, `href`, `xlink:href`, `data`, `action`, `formaction`), a value that starts with `javascript:` is removed. This applies to every element, not just iframe, because anchors and forms can also carry `javascript:` URIs.

! Opt out: `allow-unsafe`

A marker tiddler can opt out of all three rules with the field `allow-unsafe: yes`. The pragma equivalent on a `\custom` line is `_allow_unsafe=yes`.

Opt-out skips:

* The element deny list (the marker can use `object`, `frame`, etc.)
* The iframe sandbox injection (iframe renders with whatever sandbox value the marker sets, or none)
* The `javascript:` URL strip

Opt out only when you understand exactly what the marker does and trust every author who can save tiddlers with this marker tag. The opt-out is per-marker, not per-tiddler.

! Examples

!! Safe iframe with external content

```
title: vocab/my-vocab/EMBED-EXAMPLE
tags: $:/tags/CustomMarkup/Marker [[Vocab/MyVocab]]
caption: Embed example
open: EMBED
kind: word
mode: block
element: iframe
attributes: {"src": "https://example.com/widget"}
```

The plugin injects `sandbox=""`. The iframe loads the external URL but cannot run scripts. Good for visual embeds.

!! Iframe that needs scripts

```
attributes: {"src": "https://trusted.example/calculator", "sandbox": "allow-scripts"}
```

The plugin respects the explicit sandbox value. Scripts run inside the iframe, in a unique origin (no access to your wiki's DOM or cookies).

!! Opt out for legacy embed

```
allow-unsafe: yes
element: object
attributes: {"data": "media/legacy.swf", "type": "application/x-shockwave-flash"}
```

The plugin skips all checks. Use only when you trust the data source and the marker tag.

! Recommendations

* Default to the secure-by-default behaviour. Most embeds work fine with `sandbox=""`.
* If you need scripts in an iframe, set `sandbox` explicitly to the minimum required permissions. Avoid `allow-scripts allow-same-origin` together (that combination is equivalent to no sandbox).
* Do not set `allow-unsafe: yes` to "make the marker work" without understanding what it is doing. The default is the safer choice.
* Markers can be reviewed by listing all `allow-unsafe`-flagged markers in your wiki: filter `[tag[$:/tags/CustomMarkup/Marker]field:allow-unsafe[yes]]`.

